WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.332
Multi-GPU systems can increase model capacity, throughput, concurrency, or pipeline flexibility, but they do not automatically become one larger GPU.

00:00:11.332 --> 00:00:15.812
Software must divide models or work explicitly.

00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:26.617
Design around the requirement: fitting one model, accelerating one request, serving more users, processing batches, or isolating different models.

00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:32.151
Each goal favors different splitting, replication, or routing patterns.

00:00:32.151 --> 00:00:42.955
Account for slots, lanes, topology, spacing, power, cooling, VRAM, context, device balance, drivers, runtime support, communication, and thermal stability.

00:00:42.955 --> 00:00:47.435
The slowest path can dominate the system.

00:00:47.435 --> 00:00:55.605
Benchmark single-GPU and multi-GPU configurations with real prompts, context, batch, concurrency, and sustained duration.

00:00:55.605 --> 00:01:03.247
Measure quality, first token, generation, throughput, memory, utilization, temperature, power, errors, and recovery.

00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:11.417
Multi-GPU is worth it when measured workload benefit exceeds added cost and operational complexity.

00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:21.167
Sometimes a smaller model, one larger GPU, or several simple single-GPU machines is the better engineering choice.
